Pattern 1: Multi-Pointer Techniques (Fast/Slow & Gapped)
Used to locate structural landmarks (middle, cycle start, tail distance) without calculating the total length in advance.

1. The Concept

1). Fast & Slow (Floyd's Algorithm): Advance slow by 1 step and fast by 2 steps. If a cycle exists, they will eventually meet inside the loop. To find the middle of a list, when fast reaches the end, slow sits exactly at the midpoint.
2). Gapped Pointers: Advance pointer $A$ by $N$ steps ahead of pointer $B$. Then advance both by 1 step until $A$ reaches the end. Pointer $B$ is now positioned at the $N$-th node from the end.

2. Java Blueprint (Middle of List & Cycle Detection)

Java

1. Finding the Middle Node
public ListNode findMiddle(ListNode head) {
    ListNode slow = head;
    ListNode fast = head;
    
    // For odd length: slow stops at exact middle
    // For even length: slow stops at second middle node
    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
        slow = slow.next;
        fast = fast.next.next;
    }
    return slow;
}

// 2. Remove Nth Node From End of List
public ListNode removeNthFromEnd(ListNode head, int n) {
    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
    dummy.next = head;
    ListNode first = dummy;
    ListNode second = dummy;

    // Advance first pointer so the gap between first and second is 'n' nodes
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        first = first.next;
    }

    // Move both until first hits the end
    while (first != null) {
        first = first.next;
        second = second.next;
    }

    // Unlink the target node
    second.next = second.next.next;
    return dummy.next;
}

3. Essential Interview Questions
Easy: LeetCode 141 - Linked List Cycle: Basic fast/slow pointer collision detection.
Medium: LeetCode 142 - Linked List Cycle II: Once fast and slow collide, reset slow to head. Move both 1 step at a time; their next meeting point is the exact start of the cycle.
Medium: LeetCode 19 - Remove Nth Node From End of List
Medium: LeetCode 160 - Intersection of Two Linked Lists: Advance two pointers; when one hits null, redirect it to the head of the other list. They will travel equal overall lengths and collide at the intersection node.
